 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	# primary log
     2	
     3	### 00:00 HN front page: are discoursers "the kind who post a lot"?
     4	Plan: Reddit returns 403 to unauthenticated clients, so use Hacker News official APIs (Firebase + Algolia) instead.
     5	Sample = top 30 stories by points created in the last 3 days (front-page proxy). Active = commented in these threads.
     6	Discourse = wrote >=1 reply to ANOTHER user's comment that disagrees with it (hand-labelled by Claude with the rubric in src/rubric.md, before profiles are fetched).
     7	Random sample of 120 active users; up to 3 reply-pairs each are labelled. Profile per user: HN karma, account age, lifetime items/day, site-wide comments in last 3 days (Algolia).
     8	Hypothesis: discoursers post far more than non-discoursing posters (site-wide 3-day comment count ratio > 2x) and hold a larger share of comment volume than of users.
     9	Metric: median & mean site-wide 3-day comments, discoursers vs non-discoursers; bootstrap 95% CI over users (1000 resamples, seed 0). Secondary: karma, items/day, in-thread comment share.
    10	Would change my mind: ratio CI includes 1.
    11	Caveat known in advance: users with more comments have more chances to be labelled discoursers (exposure confound) -> also report the comparison restricted to users with exactly 1 labelled reply.
    12	
    13	### 00:40 Discourse vs activity on HN (final design; previous entry abandoned before profiles/analysis)
    14	Plan: Sample users via random site-wide comments in last 30 days (random timestamp -> latest comment before it), so inclusion prob ~ c_i (user's 30-day comment count) and population estimates use weights 1/c_i.
    15	D_i = binary label of that sampled comment (a uniform draw among the user's comments) using the aggressive rubric in src/rubric.md; top-level comments judged vs story title. Labelling is blind: pairs shown shuffled, without author or c.
    16	Metrics: d (weighted), beta from logistic D ~ log c (unweighted: selection is on c only, so conditional fit is unbiased), B_head, B, B0=1+CV^2, B/B0; bootstrap 95% CI over users (1000, seed 0).
    17	Hypothesis (Pew/Robertson): beta > 0, B > B0. Would change my mind: beta CI includes 0 -> bias is volume-only (1+CV^2).
    18	n ~ 120 users (time budget ~10 min). Known caveats: time-gap sampling over-weights quiet-hour comments; single labeller (Claude); HN-only.
    19	Command: python src/fetch_final.py; python src/print_blind.py; (Claude hand-labels data/final_labels.txt blind); python src/analyze_final.py
    20	Result (n=140 users, 1 labelled comment each, bootstrap 95% CI): d=0.54 [0.37,0.71] (pop-weighted; raw 0.64); beta=0.04 [-0.18,0.27];
    21	B_head=1.18 [0.92,1.62]; B=10.9 [6.6,17.1]; B0=1+CV^2=10.6 [6.7,15.7]; B/B0=1.03 [0.87,1.18]. Rate by c-quartile: 0.59/0.68/0.66/0.62.
    22	Files: results/final_users.csv, results/final_estimates.csv, figs/final_bias.png
    23	Takeaway: no evidence discoursers are heavier posters (beta CI spans 0). The ~11x voice bias is almost entirely the volume term 1+CV^2. Caveats: single labeller (Claude), aggressive rubric -> d high; HN only; time-gap sampling.

