     8	50184218,unsnap_biceps,26,False,"Today's PHP security release, and how we're strengthening security","Any idiot can create their own company. For example, I have one :)",they claim 60 full time employees,1
     9	50186018,qlte,107,False,Using thousands of AI agents to uncover new leads in biological data,"This is very, very weak.<p>Clinical science is inundated with well-reasoned &quot;potential leads&quot;. Ideas for targets, and even designing drugs to interact with those targets in the desired way, is not the bottleneck. Hasn&#x27;t been for decades. The bottleneck is in clinical development i.e. human trials.<p>There are many many AI drug discovery companies out there, many of them very well funded, and ALL of them with no shortage of &quot;potential leads&quot;.<p>These companies usually start out with the idea that their potential leads are better than the alternatives (generally because they cast a much bigger net&#x2F; considered more screening criteria than a human could, thanks to AI) and that big pharma will pay them license fees.<p>And yet, all of those companies, pretty much without exception, have turned into drug development companies themselves. They are forced to develop their own &quot;potential leads&quot; into actual approved drugs. And they are subject to the same timelines, same costs and - so far - the same probabilities of success as any other drug developer.<p>PerturbAI &amp; OpenAI&#x27;s &quot;potential leads&quot; might be better or worse than the next AI company. I would guess worse, given there is zero proprietary data involved and no clarity about why the underlying models are going to generate insightful findings. Either way, not useful.","I just saw a similar post from Anthropic which also landed like a thud. The deep need of OpenAI&#x2F;Anthropic to churn out flashy announcements <i>today</i> to with upcoming IPO is in serious conflict with the realistic pace of biological or medical research, let alone successful clinical applications. They would produce many cool leads like this which ultimately turn out to be useless before landing on the one that makes it to human clinical trials (and may fail there too).<p>A previous blog post from Anthropic used the mention of &quot;CRISPR-like&quot; to activate a mini hype cycle by association with a well-known groundbreaking discovery. Without that kind of manipulative hook these announcements are pretty thin gruel and below the bar of thousands of Pubmed papers that never made it into public view.",1
    10	50189632,r3trohack3r,7,False,mitmproxy adds --emudhra option,"I agree. We may as well make some lemonade out of these lemons. IMHO the ability of companies to &#x27;securely&#x27; close off hardware and software from their users is a sad casualty of the success of cryptography, and not a feature.",Most things have tradeoffs<p>At some point you’re forced to stack rank two or more of your values in a case where they’re mutually exclusive,0
    11	50200596,kunai,19,False,Award-Winning AI Literature,"The post is about ai literature winning awards. Since an award is supposed to be a measure of quality, not unit-cost, a trend of ai winning awards is either indicative of the quality of the writing or of a systemic flaw in how these awards are judged (which seems plausible too).<p>A van gogh painting is more valuable than a replica because 1. it is scarce and 2. owning one conveys prestige. A perfect replica of a van gogh painting conveys the same artistic expression as the original.","&gt; a trend of ai winning awards is either indicative of the quality of the writing or of a systemic flaw in how these awards are judged (which seems plausible too).<p>I definitely think this is plausible, and I think we need to start extending the idea of code &quot;smells&quot; to regular languages as well. I have TAed for literature and have reviewed many a manuscript and except for really really finely honed projects with lots of editing in post, written text almost always has many of these obvious smells (cliches, fallacies, illogical juxtapositions, poor cause and effect). AI-generated prose is no exception. Character names, phrases, descriptions of sensory experiences that are off or not quite right, improper negation (LLMs still struggles with the idea of &#x27;scope&#x27;), sentence structure being very predictable and one-dimensional (even when using the latest models and mimicking famous authors and poets and even with very careful prompting), so on and so forth, you get the idea.<p>&gt; A perfect replica of a van gogh painting conveys the same artistic expression as the original.<p>I think OP&#x27;s example was a poor one. A perfect replica of a van gogh painting indeed does convey the same artistic expression as the original. However, I think the better comparison is an understudy&#x27;s mimickry of A Starry Night which seems inspired by it and yet adds nothing to the original and is somewhat deficient in some ways, and misunderstands the artistic intent of the original. That would certainly not convey the same expression as the original, and would be met with widespread criticism.",1
    12	50200663,maybewhenthesun,16,False,What Is Gridifcation,Pfft - this doesn&#x27;t make a general point about quantisation - he completely altered the drum part. He didn&#x27;t just shift things by minute amounts to hit the &quot;exact&quot; timing while maintaining the swing. Of course if you remove swing then it&#x27;s completely changed - it could also be quantized while maintaining swing.,"It probably could. Point he&#x27;s trying to make is that it very often isn&#x27;t. Lots of  pop music is just snapped to grid. Makes it easier to repeat and swap sections.<p>I&#x27;m not sure his demonstration is &#x27;fair&#x27;, as in someone really quantizing that track would probably do a slightly better job. But it <i>does</i> highlight the effect of over-quantization. Which happens a <i>lot</i> , together with the &#x27;autotune-the-hell-out-of-it&#x27; fashion.<p>Lots of boring music being produced. But , while it wasn&#x27;t really recorded because recording was expensive,  musicians &#x27;phoning it in&#x27; have probably always been the majority of popular music.",1
    13	50201263,numpad0,189,False,Run full iOS 16.5 firmware on Android Devices,"I imagine official support for 3rd party app stores might open up some options for easily installing apps. However, that might require the store adding support for this type of iOS install or a newer version of iOS with 3rd party app store support.","Someone is going to set up an unofficial app store for this and fill it with pirated and malware-ridden apps. Then some companies will be convinced into releasing .ipsw files for &quot;debug and research&quot; purposes and starts taking subscription through those installs. Then Apple will lose choices but to support randomly downloaded .ipsw files on regular iOS devices to avoid becoming the lesser option against those, and we could slowly crawl back to the code freedom we had in 2006. Or this gets DMCA&#x27;d in 3 hours and nothing will come of it.",0
    14	50204538,vikramkr,92,False,LLMs are making formal software verification feasible at a much larger scale,"Formal verification against what?<p>The elusive 100%-coverage formal spec that covers every intended and unintended input, the interests of every stakeholder, and every possible user behavior? In the vibecoding era? No, the page has the decency to acknowledge this doesn&#x27;t exist.<p>In the end, they settle on finding crashes with a conventional fuzzer, fixing crashes, and then... writing a formal proof that the crash is fixed? That seems... pretty pointless? Am I missing something here?",The main thing they focused on in that is just cybersecurity. Formal verification to try and get rid of surface area for vulnerabilities. Seems like a good idea idk. It also sounds like they&#x27;re just kinda getting started with it - we&#x27;ll have to see what opus 5.6 or 5.7 looks like after they&#x27;ve RLed on it a bunch,1
    15	50206600,bjourne,25,False,The same AI request emits 8.5 times more CO2 in Texas than in Sweden,"Denmark would disagree, but with the limitation that the data centre needs to be sited in an appropriate location.<p><a href=""https:&#x2F;&#x2F;danskfjernvarme.dk&#x2F;viden-vaerktoejer&#x2F;udgivelser&#x2F;taenk-datacentrene-tidligt-ind-i-fjernvarmen"" rel=""nofollow"">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;danskfjernvarme.dk&#x2F;viden-vaerktoejer&#x2F;udgivelser&#x2F;taen...</a><p><a href=""https:&#x2F;&#x2F;danmarksdagblad.dk&#x2F;article&#x2F;datacenter-paa-15-millioner-kvm-kan-kobles-til-havvindmollepark-og-fjernvarmen-til-kobenhavn-38109"" rel=""nofollow"">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;danmarksdagblad.dk&#x2F;article&#x2F;datacenter-paa-15-million...</a><p><a href=""https:&#x2F;&#x2F;pulse.microsoft.com&#x2F;da-dk&#x2F;transform-da-dk&#x2F;government-da-dk&#x2F;fa1-danske-hjem-skal-opvarmes-af-nyt-datacenter&#x2F;"" rel=""nofollow"">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;pulse.microsoft.com&#x2F;da-dk&#x2F;transform-da-dk&#x2F;government...</a><p>I don&#x27;t think anywhere would want to be entirely reliant on a single datacentre, at least not at the scale of district heating use in Denmark.<p>(I gave some pages in Danish, you can search &quot;Denmark datacentre district heat&quot; yourself if you like.)",These are future plans and they lack specificity. I don&#x27;t think they will come to fruition. The problem is that waste water from data centers isn&#x27;t hot enough.,1
    16	50206723,dasyatidprime,9,False,I Did This Without Your Knowledge,"Need a human&#x27;s thumbs up before reading a wall of text from lesswrong, sorry.","If when you say “a human” you mean this is because you expect LW to be full of AI-generated text, their policy requires that chunks of language-model-written text go into special content blocks which are headed by the name of the model and rendered in a distinct sans-serif font, so that it can be used when it&#x27;s useful while making it easier for readers to filter out. <a href=""https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.lesswrong.com&#x2F;posts&#x2F;nQWavk9mnwcv6ScMR&#x2F;new-lesswrong-editor-also-an-update-to-our-llm-policy#Policy_on_LLM_Use"" rel=""nofollow"">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.lesswrong.com&#x2F;posts&#x2F;nQWavk9mnwcv6ScMR&#x2F;new-lesswr...</a>",1
