    29	50230051,frumiousirc,13,False,I don't understand quantum physics (2018),"What interview was that?<p>The famous interview I know is when he said:<p>&quot;I really can&#x27;t do a good job, any job, of explaining magnetic force in terms of something else you&#x27;re more familiar with, because I don&#x27;t understand it in terms of anything else that you&#x27;re more familiar with&quot;<p>His point was that fundamental forces cannot be understood as &quot;like&quot; everyday objects, because we don&#x27;t &quot;understand&quot; everyday objects except as applications of fundamental forces. Using those things to explain fundamental forces would just be circular. He can tell you that a stretching  rubber band is due to the electric field, but he can&#x27;t then turn around and &quot;explain&quot; that the electric field is like a stretching rubber band!
    30	Fundamental forces just are, and you understand them by studying them.","Yes, this sounds like the interview that I was remembering.<p>&gt;Fundamental forces just are, and you understand them by studying them.<p>This is largely the point I was trying to make.  One must understand QM using non-everyday ideas developed with math and measurement. But once those ideas are held, similes&#x2F;metaphor (sometimes actual isomorphs) can be useful parts of that understanding and its communication between those that share the basis for that understanding.",0
    31	50230851,matwood,79,False,"America has lost 200k of jobs in media, film and the arts. How much is AI?","If that was the only reason I could name a dozen watchable movies from the last decade, but I can&#x27;t. I can name a dozen good movies from 1984:<p>1. Beverly Hills Cop<p>2. Ghostbusters<p>3. The Terminator<p>4. Gremlins<p>5. A Nightmare on Elm Street<p>6. Police Academy<p>7. Dune<p>8. Repo Man<p>9. The NeverEnding Story<p>10. Karate Kid<p>11. Romancing the Stone<p>12. Indiana Jones and the Temple of Doom<p>Or pretty much any year from the 1980s and 1990s.","I love all those movies because they are from my generation, but how many of those would be considered good with today&#x27;s audiences?",1
    32	50236021,0c3ca83,302,False,Challenge Is Gone,"yeah, fresh air, appreciating parenting, how awful and dystopian<p>much worse than having one&#x27;s identity entirely subsumed by typing code and reading software documentation","I mean, sure, if it&#x27;s enough to satisfy a dog, why wouldn&#x27;t it be what everyone wants. Maybe the AI will even remember to play fetch with you.<p>Intellectual anything? Doing things? Pfft. Just breathe the fresh forest fire air in the fresh, new, brightly incinerated climate we&#x27;re building towards. It&#x27;s all anyone could ever aspire to leave their children.",1
    33	50236217,islewis,4,True,Show HN: Hurston-1 open source TTS that speak Southern American and Afro-English,"Hey HN, I’m open sourcing my Hurston-1 narrowband TTS model (Apache-2.0). It is very good with American English and does especially well with Southern American and Regional African English accents. Destined AI is based in Atlanta, and I built this because I’m amazed by what AI can do, and I wanted to help introduce and welcome more people to feel comfortable using and interacting with AI.<p>Since 2021, I’ve been collecting fully consented voice data from over 20,000 people, 1M audio recordings, and over 1,800 hrs of audio captured to date. For this model, I used a small part of the data, 136 hrs, to finetune Maya1 and expand it’s capabilities.<p>Hurston-1 is a narrowband TTS model that focuses the output on telephone use because that is still where most of these voices will be heard. I’ve used it to support customers such as Amazon and Salesforce and generated over half-mill revenue with it. Now I’m open sourcing it to help unlock opportunities for others as well.<p>Limitations: this model doesn’t maintain the exact same voice across prompts and doesn’t support voice cloning. For many use cases, this is fine but maybe not so - if you’re creating podcasts.<p>This is the first release - I’m already working on the next version with improvements.<p>I would appreciate, if anyone is interested in this space, download the model from huggingface, check it out and let me know any feedback. Also, open to connecting with others in this space as well.","congrats, this is a very exciting release.<p>I cannot speak to the other accents, but my experience has been that American south accents are really poorly represented in mainstream Speach-To-Text models. If your STT model is pointed at end users, this can cause quite a quality drift when looking at eval vs prod performance depending on who your user base is.<p>I am not sure what terms you have collected the speach data on, but I am sure there are lots of people working with voice models that would be very interested in this as an eval set.",0
    34	50238853,rnd0,34,False,IBM CEO says open weight models are now good and cheap on-prem,"I don&#x27;t agree with that. They made the PC revolution possible by making hardware that was at least affordable enough to be bought by a large number of businesses and individuals. It also set the stage for the clone market. I&#x27;m pretty sure that if IBM <i>really</i> set their mind to it that they could do this.<p>That doesn&#x27;t detract from your overall point that they tend to make overpriced stuff, but I don&#x27;t think they <i>have</i> to do that. Besides, what&#x27;s overpriced? NVidia&#x27;s GB300 workstation sells for 95K euros, a single RTX 6000 is now 20K. Surely even IBM can compete at that level.",That was completely accidental and they themselves tried to close the barn door with the PS&#x2F;2 line in 1987 but at that point it was too late.,1
    35	50244147,jacquesm,963,False,Eunice is almost here,"I don&#x27;t know about this guys, we used to have phones and as soon as we had the chance to use text messages instead we went all in on them. I personally, and many of the people I know avoid voice calls as much as possible.<p>So if I can&#x27;t really be bothered to speak with other humans, why would I want to speak out loud with an AI? I may be wrong and soon we&#x27;ll all have Jarvises, HALs and Samanthas but not sure the adoption will be as fast and broad as these companies want to makes us believe.",For me it is the fact that it is asynchronous that makes the one preferable over the other.,0
    36	50244753,cmiles8,102,False,Anthropic S-1,"Distilling just works too well. It costs much more to aggregate the data in the first place and train the first model, but once that exists and you are selling access, the second guy can train their model by using the first model to generate the training data.<p>The only ways to fight this is trying to either:
    37	1. Block it by cutting off access, which is pretty tough because it basically means you cannot offer unmonitored access which is a no-no to a lot of customers, and even then the workload looks very similar to actual real workloads and if the company doing the distilling is a bit sneaky and using intermediaries etc it&#x27;s almost impossible to stop, or:<p>2. Try to use IP law to block the practice, which is pretty gross given how they got the data in the first place, and so far no court has supported them on this. And even if they can make it illegal, it won&#x27;t actually help because there are countries that see having top-tier AI as a national priority and will actually support their companies breaking laws to get there.<p>People misunderstand why Anthropic is being so stingy about granting access to their top models. It&#x27;s not some kind of marketing ploy, they know that if they release them fully openly then there will be a Chinese open-weights model in 3 months that is just as good. Also the reason why they try to hide chain-of-thought and the probability distributions.",Given Anthropic is basically just “distilling” the IP of others they’re in no position to cry foul when others run the same playbook on Anthropic IP. They can pound sand on that front.,1
    38	50248159,anticorporate,16,True,CSS-Tricks has a new owner,CSS-Tricks has a new owner,"I wish I didn&#x27;t have such a bleak outlook for community-focused tech publishing. Sites like LWN and 404 Media seem to be hanging on, but they&#x27;re more likely the exceptions that prove the rule.<p>My paycheck for the better part of a decade came from publications run by a large tech company in an arrangement not too dissimilar from DO&#x27;s relationship with CSS-Tricks. It was great while it lasted, but shortly after I left it was the first thing on the chopping block when the layoffs started. That was 3-4 years ago and the landscape for quality tech publishing hasn&#x27;t gotten any easier.",0
    39	50252068,tyrabound,40,False,Microsoft offers mass buyouts for roughly a quarter of its workforce,"The big tech companies are getting old. I think it&#x27;s a fundamental rule that the competence and effectiveness of an org <i>must</i> fall, over time, driven by the fact that a person will more readily hire someone they can <i>understand</i> than someone that is more intelligent&#x2F;competent&#x2F;effective. This causes a slow degradation.<p>I also think there&#x27;s been a <i>drastic</i> shift in the working culture of tech&#x2F;engineering spaces, in general, over the last 15 years. From my experience, it&#x27;s definitely drifted away from a bunch of nerds solving problems to something more towards emotional&#x2F;personal&#x2F;politics. I recently saw someone cry when a person <i>very kindly</i> asked them to explain a discrepancy. &quot;Back in my day&quot; people would just state facts, point out flaws, and argue a bit, and have fun doing it, for better or worse.","I suspect there is still a good amount of shedding the defensive-hiring going on from the pre-AI, low interest, high growth inflation front-loading era. Now all that is crumbling, interest is rising, growth is contracting due to several factors, and AI is clearly crushing manpower demands. What do you do under these new circumstances with an excess of staff you largely hired as capacity and defensively?<p>It may be a bit controversial, but I believe the male tectonic shifts going in right now under our feet are also driving much of the shift regarding “immigration” because billionaires don’t need immigrants as much anymore and they’re also causing systemic problems in things like the housing market. In fact from what I’m hearing, there is concern that immigration is now becoming a potential cost due to the major changes in society rather than a profit center by scaling up marginal returns from every immigrant you can import by hook or crook, i.e., the more people you import, the more you can undermine salaries and increase revenues and thus, profits.",0
    40	50252531,zoeysmithe,23,False,Oldest GUI application that still runs on Windows 11,"One crucial part of my role as my family&#x27;s de facto IT support was making sure my mom&#x27;s FreeCell score record (which was just a Windows registry value) made it from each computer to the next across countless upgrades. She hated the new version that came out somewhere along the way because the card decks changed. (Windows 7? Windows 10? I switched to Linux full time in the XP era so my memory is a little fuzzy.) So I made sure to keep a backup of the Win32 version she was used to, and loaded it onto every machine she ever owned. It wasn&#x27;t quite the 33 years of its lifetime, but probably close to 30.<p>Mom passed earlier this year, so the FreeCell score record won&#x27;t ever increment again, but I still made a point to back it up one last time when going through her desktop this summer.","I&#x27;m so sorry for your loss, but now you have to tell us her high score!",0
