    53	50302071,wallst07,79,False,Binance-hacker-1029 – $5.6B stolen from Binance,"Nope, that engineer had far too much access. I&#x27;m confident this is the case in pretty much all organizations to some degree, but a lot of engineers are also the only ones that can deny themselves the access so in a roundabout way it&#x27;s their fault anyway.<p>But a lot (generalizing here) don&#x27;t like giving up access &quot;in case they need it&quot;. I haven&#x27;t worked backend in ages, but years ago there was one developer that went to the ops guys and said &quot;can I just ssh to production I need to check some env vars to debug my software&quot; (or something to that degree). Ops team was good and it was a hard no - &quot;just add some logging, you can deploy a new release quickly enough&quot;.","It&#x27;s not a access thing, it&#x27;s a security control.<p>For example at most good crypto companies that hold wallets it&#x27;s not possible to ssh at all, even if someone asked and go permission. It just simply isn&#x27;t possible short of having a AWS root key or physical access to a computer in a DC.<p>There is no login, no accounts, no ssh keys or similar, they are immutable containers running from images with a signed SHA. The SHA can only be built by a build system that has it&#x27;s own set of strict security controls.<p>Without details, I don&#x27;t know why a comproised laptop would make any difference unless it submitted code through a PR and had it approved by multiple people.",1
