    85	50324623,Twey,20,False,"Sanstun: A sans-IO, parallel-friendly WireGuard implementation in Rust","&gt; Sure, the same can be said of an async library<p>An generic future is much harder to be called like this. You need to to create wakers and if you don&#x27;t properly handle them you&#x27;ll busy loop. You also need to provide the exact runtime the future needs for its I&#x2F;O.<p>&gt; so long as you&#x27;re okay with none of your other flows being able to make progress in the meantime<p>And isn&#x27;t that what blocking code does all the time?<p>&gt; ‘The best of both worlds’ means that it shares the advantages of both<p>If you look at it from the perspective of a caller:<p>- you can easily use it from blocking code without needing a runtime
    86	- you can easily use it from async code without blocking the executor<p>As such it does provide the best of both worlds to the caller.<p>It is true that it&#x27;s the worst of both worlds for who is writing the Sans-IO library though.<p>&gt; or, as we used to call it, pure functional programming<p>You can for sure describe this as &quot;pure&quot; and &quot;functional&quot;, but I bet most people think of a different paradigm when talking about pure functional programming.","&gt; An generic future is much harder to be called like this. You need to to create wakers and if you don&#x27;t properly handle them you&#x27;ll busy loop. You also need to provide the exact runtime the future needs for its I&#x2F;O.<p>You don&#x27;t actually have to handle I&#x2F;O with the async runtime if you don&#x27;t want. You _could_ just poll the futures yourself in a loop, which is roughly the same as what happens if you just call the transition function in a loop for a manual state machine. And likewise if you just keep calling the transition function with empty input you&#x27;ll also busy-loop.<p>&gt; And isn&#x27;t that what blocking code does all the time?<p>Sure, if you have only one flow you care about or you have threads to take care of your concurrency, this is fine. You can do it with any model (blocking, async, manual state machine).<p>&gt; - you can easily use it from blocking code without needing a runtime - you can easily use it from async code without blocking the executor<p>But these things are already true of a state machine compiled from async&#x2F;await instead of written by hand. `poll()` is just a function, and you can call it where you like. By the contract of `Future`, just like the contract of the state machine transition, it&#x27;s guaranteed to ‘not block’.  The waker machinery exists to help you write engines that decide when to poll, but if you&#x27;ve got your own external notion of that you&#x27;re free to just call `poll()`. The standard library even has some optimization around this case, e.g. `Waker::noop()`.<p>&gt; You can for sure describe this as &quot;pure&quot; and &quot;functional&quot;, but I bet most people think of a different paradigm when talking about pure functional programming.<p>I agree, but it&#x27;s confusing to me that this is the case. Original pure functional programming was all about ‘how can we write more useful programs without any effects’ and ‘what benefits can we get from doing so’. I think those questions are kind of answered; since Moggi&#x27;s work in the early nineties it&#x27;s been focused more on ‘how can we keep the benefits of pure functions while also being able to encode effectful logic?’, to which manually-written state machines are a rather primitive sort of answer. They bear pretty strong resemblance to iteratees&#x2F;transducers&#x2F;pipes&#x2F;etc. from that lineage, except that those things aim to build a higher-level combinator language on top to reduce the amount of manual state-machine writing one has to do.",1
    87	50331185,abstractspoon,45,True,"Why I Still Own a DVD Player – and You Should, Too","Why I Still Own a DVD Player – and You Should, Too","I watch only tubi.com and sbs.com.au. When ads come on I just mute the sound, gaze at the ceiling, and ponder some programming related task I&#x27;m working on. I might ponder the same issue 10 times, but the benefit of that is by the following day I&#x27;ve cleared every wrinkle and work proceeds effortlessly. YMMV",0
    88	50333201,bayarearefugee,25,False,AI fan wikis riddled with errors are muddying the waters of games culture,"So what&#x27;s the solution? I imagine the right one isn&#x27;t specific to fan wikis. We&#x27;re going to need sites where 100% of contributed content is human-written. Who is building this? How will it work?<p>If the solution merely makes it high friction to post AI content that is probably good enough. In other words if someone has to copy&#x2F;paste the output of an LLM into a text field and even then the tech detects that and throws an error that would be a big step in the right direction.<p>Reddit, Wikipedia, fan wikis, reviews, Hacker News comments - I&#x27;m sure there are a dozen more categories I&#x27;m missing.",&gt; So what&#x27;s the solution?<p>There is no solution (though there will be plenty of grifters trying to sell one).<p>The open internet we currently know will be lost to mountains of slop just like usenet and email were even prior to the AI era.,1
    89	50333360,tptacek,363,False,"America on Data Centers: listen to 10,000+ city council comments","While the segment of the population who are motivated and able to engage with the public comment process may often not be representative demographically, it&#x27;s hideous (besides semantically inaccurate) to dismiss them as a special interest group.<p>It also doesn&#x27;t make sense to include negativity and positivity as part of representation. Yes, people feel more strongly about bad things than good things, but that&#x27;s everybody. It would be a mistake to try to excise that. It&#x27;s already our job to account for it.<p>From your tone, this seems less like an opinion on democratic process and more like you just happen to have the opposite opinions of most people in the aforementioned segment, statistically, and you (perhaps understandably) are angered by them, and you see a viable argument for reducing their voices.","They are definitionally a concentrated special interest group. If that&#x27;s hideous to you, that&#x27;s on you.",1
    90	50335890,Joker_vD,71,False,Show HN: I've updated my Linux System Call website and API,I&#x27;m obviously not going to discuss this argument further.<p>Trying to counter a logical argument with a technical argument is pretty lame and doesn&#x27;t make for a healthy conversation.,"Uh, sorry, what? Trying to measure similarity between syscalls is a) impossible, b) pointless, so obviously I&#x27;d really like to understand why someone would want to do something like this — but I&#x27;d rather not speculate about what and why you think and believe, and instead to just straight hear from you.<p>If you&#x27;re interested in why I think what I think — there&#x27;s a sibling comment where I tried to articulate my position. I&#x27;d like to hear about yours, if you don&#x27;t mind.<p>P.S. You didn&#x27;t actually offer any argument, logical (?) or technical (?!), in your original comment, and I think you&#x27;re actually have the meaning of those words reversed: &quot;logical argument&quot; appeals to logical relations between the abstract concepts, while &quot;technical argument&quot; appeals to the underlying technology (the kernel implementation in our case) and tries to deal with particularities of reality. So if anything, my argument was the logical (semantical?) one, not that it really matters.",1
    91	50341986,mattbrewsbytes,27,False,"Anthropic's internal AI use, at retail prices, now exceeds its employee pay","No. This is opportunity cost.<p>A clear example is Google. They can easily give all compute to Deepmind, yet majority is allocated to be sold at market rate.<p>So, every compute Deepmind and Googlers get is lost revenue opportunity and must be measured as such.<p>No other internal tools or servers has this much external demand to be comparable.<p>Also, Anthropic is showing that to operate at Anthropic level, you need $300,000 compute spend per employee","There is a huge difference between Anthropic and Google dog-fooding: Anthropic does not own or build data centers, they rent (as far as all the reporting on this I&#x27;ve read). Anthropic employees dog-fooding with Claude tools isn&#x27;t &quot;internal&quot;, its most likely being run on external AI data center compute, which they have to pay for, there&#x27;s just no margin on it.  It might even be run out of Google&#x27;s data centers.",1
    92	50344709,xienze,18,False,"Valve ""played capitalism so well they broke it"" and now Steam can't be replaced",PC Gamers literally just don&#x27;t want to use a second launcher so it doesn&#x27;t matter what you offer them. See how Epic offered games for free and it still didn&#x27;t matter.,"There&#x27;s a little more to it than &quot;just a launcher.&quot; My Steam account dates back to the release of Half-Life 2. Loads of games, thousands of dollars spent, and a service that&#x27;s been reliable for over 20 years. Why would I use a different service? I&#x27;m not unique, everyone else using Steam is in the same boat, just with less time invested.<p>They&#x27;ve undoubtedly locked us all in, but they&#x27;ve shown themselves to be trustworthy and reliable.",1
    93	50345448,darth_avocado,46,False,"Valve ""played capitalism so well they broke it"" and now Steam can't be replaced",PC Gamers literally just don&#x27;t want to use a second launcher so it doesn&#x27;t matter what you offer them. See how Epic offered games for free and it still didn&#x27;t matter.,"&gt; PC Gamers literally just don&#x27;t want to use a second launcher<p>Is that true? And if it is true, why is that?<p>Pointing to free games on Epic and non usage tells you that people don’t want Epic. Doesn’t tell you why. Nor does it tell you whether people continue to stick with Valve. And that is the point in my original comment.<p>If Valve isn’t good, you can’t just say people won’t go for another better option “just because”.",1
