    97	50361484,fwipsy,22,False,"Stringman, an $800 open-source room scale gripper robot",I kind of want to try something like this for garden weeding. Put a small spinning blade instead of a grabber arm on it. Computer vision can identify any weeds popping up and it&#x27;ll go over and cut them before they get too big&#x2F;woody for the blades. The challenge would be dodging taller plants and probably the forces that the blade would put on it.,"Why not use grippers to pull them? Probably more effective because there&#x27;s no root to grow back from, shouldn&#x27;t require too much force when they&#x27;re small&#x2F;the soil is damp, and you don&#x27;t have to figure out torsion&#x2F;&quot;friendly fire&quot; from a spinning blade.",1
    98	50361774,bigbadfeline,71,False,What Millions of Data Points Reveal About US Inflation,"One shouldn&#x27;t forget is that every dollar that&#x27;s spent on something, is a dollar not spent on something else. As long as both options are domestic and does not involve money being sent abroad, increase in price of one product means decrease of price in another, so in a way, gas prices cannot move overall inflation at all: gas, becoming more expensive, made other things cheaper by suppressing demand on them.<p>In the old days, oil in the US was mostly imported, so that wasn&#x27;t the case: higher oil price meant just less money left for anything else, so economy could fall while prices rose (stagflation). Now, this is no longer the case, higher oil prices mean more money for everyone because US is a net exporter.","&gt; gas, becoming more expensive, made other things cheaper by suppressing demand on them.<p>Nope, it may suppress demand for &quot;other things&quot; but doesn&#x27;t make them cheaper. The making of &quot;other things&quot; uses gas too, as do their makers, so they have to <i>increase</i> their prices as well in order to compensate for the increased input costs.<p>The suppression of demand, if it materializes at all, has a natural floor, as does the reduction of margin - it&#x27;s natural floor is 0 - past that, reduction of price is impossible. In fact, reduced output usually leads to higher <i>costs per  unit produced</i> due to reduced economies of scale, as a result, lower demand often results in higher prices <i>even if gas prices stay the same</i>.",1
    99	50362973,Shitty-kitty,43,False,AI stocks slide as Texas governor result raises data center risks,"It will be interesting to see if and how people who campaigned on broad &quot;anti-datacenter&quot; platforms actually make it happen. I imagine it&#x27;s easy to take a stance but getting the real political capital together, especially as a governor who will almost surely need their legislature, when it means turning down jobs—few as the ongoing ones might be—and real economic development. I hope it works because it potentially sets a precedent and maybe a legal framework for a state to actually say &quot;we don&#x27;t want this kind of business here&quot; for things that aren&#x27;t just data centers.","Legislation or not, good luck getting those permits.",1
   100	50365550,hogs_get_fat,4,False,Geoffrey Hinton: it might turn out we were better off without AI,"&gt; If it weren’t OAI and Anthropic, it would be others.<p>Doing what? Rushing to make trillion dollar companies, rather then developing the tech and their companies in a sustainable way? I don&#x27;t even know what in the original comment this would be a response to.","Rushing towards creating machines that can give you vast amounts of military, political or economic power without regard to it&#x27;s effects on the old order.<p>The particular form of the pursuit of power practiced by OAI and Antrophic - the creation of trillion dollar companies, is one of the most benign in recorded history. It&#x27;s so unnaturally benign, that I doubt it&#x27;s going to survive for much longer.",1
   101	50366400,giwook,61,True,"Tell HN: Gemini trains on chat history, even for subscribers (personal accounts)","If you have a personal Google AI subscription, all your saved chat history in Gemini can be used for training and there is no way to opt out unless you turn off chat history or use temporary chat sessions. These chat transcripts may even be read by human reviewers. Although Google claims to disconnect them from your account when human-reviewed, they explicitly warn <i>not to enter confidential information</i>.<p>https:&#x2F;&#x2F;support.google.com&#x2F;gemini&#x2F;answer&#x2F;16836988<p><i>“[Gemini app activity] is used to improve Google services for everyone, including by training generative AI models, if your Keep Activity setting is on.“</i><p>https:&#x2F;&#x2F;support.google.com&#x2F;gemini&#x2F;answer&#x2F;13594961?hl=en#human_review<p><i>“A subset of chats are reviewed by human reviewers”</i><p>https:&#x2F;&#x2F;support.google.com&#x2F;gemini&#x2F;answer&#x2F;13594961?hl=en#pn_retain_data<p><i>“Chats reviewed by human reviewers [...] are not deleted when you delete your activity. Instead, they are retained for up to three years.”</i><p>https:&#x2F;&#x2F;support.google.com&#x2F;gemini&#x2F;answer&#x2F;13594961?hl=en#pn_data_usage<p><i>“Please don’t enter confidential information that you wouldn’t want a reviewer to see or Google to use to improve our services, including machine-learning technologies.”</i><p>To disable their training on your chat sessions, you need to turn off “Keep Activity” in Gemini’s settings. Turning that off means you’ll lose any chat sessions after 72 hours.<p>I learned this too late, thinking that having a paid subscription = not being turned into the product. Apparently paying is not enough.<p>Thank you to this user on HN for mentioning in another thread: https:&#x2F;&#x2F;news.ycombinator.com&#x2F;item?id=50355424","Is this really surprising to anyone?<p>I just sorta assumed that Google was hoovering up anything I type into its AI and don&#x27;t tell it anything too personal or sensitive.<p>Is anyone under the illusion that our data is not being collected, whether it&#x27;s Google or literally even the seemingly innocuous TVs in our home that have been found to be spying on us 24&#x2F;7?",1
   102	50371237,giancarlostoro,189,False,AI is forcing us to pay off 40 years of technical debt,"...because Mainframes were not thought for Devs &amp; Tech People, but Business and Compliance Clowns enabled to do some stuff without CS degrees :-D","Sure, but IBM could have changed all of that and made mainframes drastically more approachable and viable.",1
   103	50372890,JeppyG,15,True,"Show HN: After a decade of reversing, I got co-op working in DOS Wing Commander","Show HN: After a decade of reversing, I got co-op working in DOS Wing Commander","Incredible. Honestly tweaking old games is one of my favorite things to do with AI. I&#x27;ve been having a huge go at Captain Claw, one of my favorite games from childhood. Thanks for sharing this. :)",0
   104	50373002,zahlman,365,False,How I use AI (Oct 2026),"I think there are a lot of people out there with good problem-solving skills but for whatever reason did not get into programming or they tried and they got put off with learning the languages &#x2F; libraries or got stuck and didn&#x27;t find a solution easily enough and gave up etc.<p>With a sufficiently good LLM these people will skip the tedious part of programming that most of us had the motivation &#x2F; patience to go through. All the folks saying that actually writing code wasn&#x27;t the bottleneck have had a LOT of years to get to that point, years a lot of people aren&#x27;t interested in going through.<p>I&#x27;m not saying lay people will be able to join mega projects and work at FAANG. I&#x27;m saying a sufficiently courageous lay person will be able to make an app to automate whatever tedious birocratic process their niche work hasn&#x27;t seen a product yet.","I can absolutely believe this, actually. Considering how the access to rapid prototyping has rejuvenated my interest in programming when I <i>am</i> a seasoned programmer, I definitely see the value in that kind of jump-start.<p>And yeah, without LLMs, writing code absolutely <i>can</i> be the bottleneck sometimes, if only for psychological reasons. I&#x27;ve definitely experienced something like &quot;coder&#x27;s block&quot; before. An anti-flow state, if you will.<p>(But skydhash is also right. The &quot;unknown unknowns&quot; are a big deal and there&#x27;s only so much an LLM can do about that, at least unless the user is actively aware of the general shape of that problem and trying to learn via the LLM and fill in those knowledge gaps.)",0
   105	50373232,basilikum,55,False,AMD expects to outsell Intel in Q4 for the first time in history,"If you assume the machine will be compromised, is there any way around this?","Confidential computing and hardware security are starting to be an actual thing now. They are mostly used for DRM and I would not trust them when your adversary is three letters long.<p>Hardware security used to be a joke. Now verified boot, encrypted RAM, key storage in hardware security models and hardware and OS attestation are getting somewhere.<p>But if you&#x27;re important enough of a target to a state actor they will magically have some workaround. For your average raid  on your home they might actually work depending on the specific setup.<p>For really high threat models you want zero trust models with E2EE as well as staying anonymous so no one knows where the hardware (and the people which might be much more vulnerable) are located. Tor exists for a reason.",0
   106	50374436,somenameforme,208,False,US voters roundly reject data centers in ballot measures,"&gt;&gt; if somebody&#x27;s only concerned about paying rent next month<p>If this is true wouldn’t it be nice if the municipal budget had some room to manouver? [1]<p>[1]: <a href=""https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.forbes.com&#x2F;sites&#x2F;billconerly&#x2F;2026&#x2F;09&#x2F;19&#x2F;700-billion-data-center-boom-tax-gains-beat-political-backlash&#x2F;"" rel=""nofollow"">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.forbes.com&#x2F;sites&#x2F;billconerly&#x2F;2026&#x2F;09&#x2F;19&#x2F;700-bill...</a>","Let me translate this for how a person negatively affected by this is going to see what you&#x27;re saying:<p>&#x27;We are regularly deceptive and unadulteratedly self interested. Now we&#x27;re going to create a catastrophic problem for you, one that is reasonably likely to be unprecedented in scale, because we deem it to be profitable for us. But don&#x27;t worry, we promise to throw you a bone in the future to make it a bit more tolerable. Deal?&#x27;",1
