   119	50398793,figmert,19,False,We've built an open-source feature flag server for 11 years,"Eh, I built a feature flagging system for service with millions of daily active users and it: wasn&#x27;t hard to do, was easy to extend to support targeting, segments, and progressive rollout, and on top of it flags are easy to cache, so the database pressure was not that big. We did this long before LLMs, and it occupied maybe a month of engineering time for me, here and there as we needed new features, while I was doing other things. We did choose the wrong hash for bucketing in the beginning, but we noticed the stairstep patterns in our buckets quickly (an advantage of having millions of requests per day to measure) and picked a better one. It was much better this way since it meant backend flags without a roundtrip to a 3rd party service (that was the main motivation, we wanted this kind of control without adding latency to our requests). I remain baffled by launch darkly or other such services. And today, with LLMs, just build it yourself is hard to argue against.","As I mentioned, I&#x27;m not advocating for LD. I&#x27;m advocating for feature management systems (over env vars&#x2F;db values and other similar naive implementations). The build vs buy is a separate argument.<p>&gt; that was the main motivation, we wanted this kind of control without adding latency to our requests<p>Right, and most, if not all, off the shelf feature management systems have this problem solved already. For example LD uses a streaming architecture by default to push out flags to your application within 200ms, for client facing applications (android&#x2F;ios&#x2F;web), flags were retrieved once on load from a CDN, keeping the rest of your application snappy. I believe Unleash also has some sort of streaming architecture.",1
   120	50403974,cyanydeez,266,False,Britain to make salary disclosure in job ads mandatory,Maybe I misunderstood but are you saying that companies have these lengthy interview procedures to filter out smart&#x2F;capable&#x2F;non-desperate candidates?<p>Sounds a bit counterproductive,"I&#x27;m saying business managers tend to follow patterns that gets them to the numbers on their paper. What you, or a single employee, sees as counter productive is often just a means to an end.<p>They&#x27;ll spend 5x your ask for a raise finding someone to fill your position because denying you keeps the rest of their payroll in line. And if you consider these people communicate across their industries, they will make informal cartels that depress all employee wages.<p>So I wouldn&#x27;t be surprised if there was just a natural way to filter our employees who would be to demanding by making them jump through more hoops.",1
   121	50404140,zyx321,1,False,Upload Your RAM,"I suspect that this actually downloads something instead of uploading. I have no proof, but I believe it.",It&#x27;s a reference to the old &quot;download more RAM&quot; meme.<p>I would be shocked if it did anything at all.,1
   122	50407488,wolfinterval,17,False,Anthropic IPO buyers see valuation as low as $1.1T,I don&#x27;t believe they literally meant Tulips but rather its hype cycle. I commented on the other two that can be taken literally,"The interesting thing is I think we might over hype the tulip hype now.<p>It seems like much of the tulip mania has become the stuff of legend.<p>From what I have read, it seems closer to baseball card trading than AI or dot com.<p>You can find baseball and pokemon cards trading for thousands of dollars right now on ebay.<p>This would seem insane to a culture just valueing the cards as pieces of cardboard.<p>It would be like hundred of years from now people viewing our society as wanting to buy Mickey Mantle cards instead of houses. It is a very small number of people involved with this. It is not a widespread movement to hold your retirement money in Mickey Mantle cards.<p>In an interesting way it shows even better though he we are susceptible to these type of mythologies. People don&#x27;t even question the absurd givens of the narratives of the tulip mania. We just repeat these narratives to each other as if they are historic fact.",1
   123	50410467,nianzhibai,2,True,"ZapFast: A fast, native WhatsApp client in Rust and egui","ZapFast: A fast, native WhatsApp client in Rust and egui",It&#x27;s a bit basic. I installed it and then uninstalled it.,1
   124	50412455,carschno,5,True,Petered,Petered,"I think the Peter Principle addresses a good point, but misses a critical one: the hierarchy itself. Obviously, a manager requires different skills than an engineer, so why should a good engineer be a good manager? This holds the other way round: a good manager is not particularly likely to be a good engineer.<p>Both is fine, these are just different jobs. However, the problem is that the manager is &#x27;above&#x27; the engineer in the generally accepted hierarchy. Consequently, engineers are pressured to become managers for the development. As long as this hierarchy exists, the problem will exist.",1
   125	50421148,nicbou,48,True,Ask HN: What are your advice for a CS/IT student for the future?,Hello. I&#x27;m an Information Technology university student from Greece. I&#x27;m older than most of my peers and i feel anxious about my professional future with all the advancements of AI. What advice do you have for someone who just starting out?,"Do not tie your entire identity to your job and how well you do it. Learn to appreciate the world through non-technical lenses and develop a life and hobbies outside of tech.<p>AI is currently killing my business and threatening my old CS career, but I have things to retreat to: a network, friends, hobbies, nature, art. I find answers in the countless books and essays I have read, instead of flying blind. Liberal arts matter.<p>I did not see the use of those things when I was laser-focused on my career, but they added so much richness to my life outside of work, and now they&#x27;re the only thing between me and despair.<p>That aside, do not neglect networking, negotiation and other soft skills. They do much more for your career than being a really solid coder. Software engineering is engineering. It requires herding a bunch of human beings to achieve unclear, changing goals. Code is just an artefact of that endeavour.",0
