   129	50437292,harishbala,10,True,Tell HN: Some of My Recent Ideas,"I thought it&#x27;ll be fun to share some different ideas that I&#x27;ve had AI build in the last couple of weeks or months. Using my local Qwen 27b model.<p>I had AI build a Roku remote in the web and connect to my jelly fin so I can easily search for shows and autoplay them on my Roku. Also features an API and I can scan an NFC tag and it will automatically open a video on my Roku.<p>I have an HD home run so I had AI build me a DVR page, it could use some work but overall it&#x27;s working and it&#x27;s pretty cool.<p>I&#x27;ve been building a health ecosystem tracking food journaling about health, and then adding NFC tags to different medications and supplements. I had to build a Android app that sinks any of my NFC tag scans automatically to my server. Since I don&#x27;t run tailscale or anything like that to get in and out of my network.<p>I have a game boy color cartridge with SD card. I thought it would be fun to add ebooks. Haven&#x27;t tested this one yet but in theory I&#x27;ll be able to copy books onto my game boy color cartridge and then use my game boy Pocket to read books lol.<p>I have a Ricoh theta S, that used to have an app, but doesn&#x27;t appear to work with latest Ricoh apps lately. So it&#x27;s building me an app so I can take 360 photos, and embed gps, and time&#x2F;date info easily. (Basically connect to my phone so it happens automatically).<p>I have a handful more but I&#x27;ll stop there for now.<p>Got any fun ideas you&#x27;ve been playing with?<p>(I&#x27;m not building any of these for money, they are for my own personal enjoyment).<p>I might post a spec&#x2F;prompt to my blog for these at some point but for now this is all you get.","The health idea of yours is really interesting imo lol.<p>I’ve been playing with two:
   130	1. Knowledge Graph that auto parses vital context out
   131	2. A security feature for agentic memory to secure chunks that don’t determine perms in memory<p>Would love to chat with anyone on these lol",0
   132	50438348,andai,296,False,Fable 5.2 one-shotted my nephew's dream game,"Ooooof the results here are <i>rough</i>. A very young child might find this charming, but I think it&#x27;s kind of embarrassing. Like, the walk cycles alone are. Yeah...<p>To say nothing of ur word shaders, inconsistency in the characters, questionable gender stereotypes, etc.<p>This definitely tells me that AI definitely isn&#x27;t there yet.",When it gets there eventually (5-10 years??) it&#x27;s gonna be pretty rough for the humans that used to do those things. Then again I suppose that applies to the rest of the economy as well.,0
   133	50440446,m_w_,8,False,AlphaProof Code,Noob Question: Apart from these cryptographic systems would it be useful to build these proofs for general pieces of software? Specifically:<p>1. Has someone applied it to software systems where an AI agent is in the critical path in production i.e. non-deterministic action may occur(albeit with recovery)?<p>2. What scenarios does it make sense to have a lean proof from the start? Like i imagine if you wanted to make a database system with some guarantees around ACID then it might be better having this from the get go. But probably not that useful for building an API backed service.,"&gt; But probably not that useful for building an API backed service.<p>At least in domains that are &quot;important&quot; (and perhaps everyone thinks their business&#x2F;API is important?), I&#x27;d want this.<p>For example - Stripe. If I&#x27;m using the stripe API, I want it to be guaranteed to work exactly as documented in all scenarios, given that money is at stake?<p>Getting the Stripe API written in Lean is a different challenge, of course.",1
   134	50443051,sublinear,147,False,Writing as a thinking tool,"I was referring to written communication. I agree with<p>&gt; Words are most effective when they buck the trend in just the right way.<p>But I cant bridge from conference room to writing style.<p>Also my aim is not to produce Hemingway style writing, my goal is to produce writing better than I did yesterday.","Yeah... I don&#x27;t think Hemingway ever gave a damn about anyone else, especially the reader.<p>Anyway, I&#x27;m just saying that persuasion is half the job. LLMs can&#x27;t produce new information. They rearrange and distill existing information. That doesn&#x27;t convince anyone who is already at least partially familiar with the topic at hand.<p>A good writer knows how to build and communicate mental models or anything else that would make a topic more comfortable. They also understand the persuasiveness of novelty. If you were trying to explain higher dimensions, are you <i>really</i> just going to bring up Flatland every time? Probably not, but that&#x27;s what an LLM does because it&#x27;s been trained on garbage written by teenagers on reddit.",1
   135	50456473,zaius,1,True,"Show HN: Yogurt – Free, private, open-source alternative to Granola","Hey HN, I’m Eric and I want to share an OSS project that I’ve been building in my free time. Yogurt is a free, MIT licensed desktop app that uses on-device AI models to transcribe, summarize, and chat with your meetings.<p>Here&#x27;s a quick demo video: <a href=""https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.youtube.com&#x2F;watch?v=BFfbXAwRq98"" rel=""nofollow"">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.youtube.com&#x2F;watch?v=BFfbXAwRq98</a><p>Similar to Granola, Yogurt runs in the background and does not join your calls. But unlike Granola, Yogurt is open-source, free to use, and private. Your meeting transcripts, call recordings, and summaries never leave your computer.<p>As small OSS models have gotten faster and more powerful, Yogurt seemed like a pretty obvious win for privacy and cost conscious folks. You do not need a frontier model to summarize meeting notes or answer questions about them. And if you want to make your notes available to a frontier model, you can just share it with Anthropic, OpenAI, etc. directly via Yogurt’s local MCP server. There’s no need to use a paid cloud service as a middleman.<p>I tried some of the most popular OSS note taking tools, and most were missing features like calendar integration, MCP server, etc. or required you to connect or create an account. And more than that, they didn’t feel polished enough for it to be something I actually wanted to use daily, and so I built Yogurt to scratch that itch. I spent a lot of time fine-tuning every UI interaction&#x2F;animation and optimizing the model calls to get the most out of your hardware + make it snappy.<p>To support Windows&#x2F;Mac&#x2F;Linux, I decided to go with Electron. I use whisper.cpp for transcription and Apple’s on-device SpeechAnalyzer if your system supports it. For call summaries and questions, I use a local open weight LLM that automatically gets downloaded and set up based on what your hardware can handle without your cooling fans firing off like crazy (this part uses llama.cpp).<p>I’ve spent a lot of time working on details like echo detection&#x2F;reduction, global&#x2F;folder&#x2F;local level search respectively, and auto-end recordings. Hope this is useful for any of you who have held out on using AI note tools for privacy or cost reasons, or for folks using cloud tools who want to switch to something free and open source that still feels great to use.<p>Would love to hear what you think about the onboarding and note taking experience :)","I also got frustrated with Granola earlier this year and have been building my replacement - Noted: <a href=""https:&#x2F;&#x2F;takenoted.com&#x2F;"" rel=""nofollow"">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;takenoted.com&#x2F;</a><p>It shares similar principles but is (currently) closed source and mac-only.<p>Take a look at anarlog as well - it&#x27;s a great project and I wish I&#x27;d found it earlier as I probably would have contributed instead of starting my own: <a href=""https:&#x2F;&#x2F;anarlog.so&#x2F;"" rel=""nofollow"">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;anarlog.so&#x2F;</a><p>Let me know if you want to compare notes - I am sure we have hit a lot of the same issues.",0
