   154	50499424,philipallstar,64,False,The Claude Delusion,"Do you really believe the majority of people are indoctrinated and have no valid thoughts or feelings on these things?<p>If yes, what about you, compared to them, makes you immune to indoctrination?<p>My main counterpoint datum is the US midterm election exit polls. People said affordability and corruption are their top issues <i>and</i> that they are interconnected. Tech bros are their exemplar de jour - with good reason.","&gt; Do you really believe the majority of people are indoctrinated and have no valid thoughts or feelings on these things?<p>If half the population consume the same sort of media that&#x27;s all saying the same thing then they&#x27;re very likely to believe the same thing. They will believe they noticed something, when in fact they were told it over and over. Pick whichever half you like. Just as I don&#x27;t trust a nationalist&#x27;s opinion on immigration, I don&#x27;t trust a Marxist&#x27;s opinion on the wealthy.<p>As for &quot;valid feelings&quot; I agree this is about feelings and not thoughts, but I don&#x27;t know what a valid or invalid feeling is.",1
   155	50503076,GolfPopper,81,False,SpaceX catches the Starship to end Flight 15,"The problem isn’t “it’s more complicated” but more “if something goes wrong there’s not a lot you can do”. There’s basically no redundancy and a lot of single points of failure. Having two sets of Parachutes is a lot harder to screw up than a powered landing.<p>With a normal capsule, you have multiple fuel tanks and redundant RCS thrusters so you’ll practically always be able to deorbit and land (since you also have redundant parachutes). If starship has a fuel leak, how are you going to land, even if you’re able to deorbit?","If they reach the launch cadence they&#x27;re aiming for, the fix for a lot of things is going to become &quot;just send another Ship&quot;.",1
   156	50503212,nine_k,68,False,SpaceX catches the Starship to end Flight 15,"&gt; Starship’s been in development for over two decades<p>What? The first version of something that looks anything like Starship was the ITS announced back in 2016, later &quot;BFR&quot; in 2017 and the stainless steel version (which is now flying) in December 2018.<p>Eight years is very reasonable for a fully reusable heavy lift system - something <i>no one</i> has built before.<p>The Space Shuttle took roughly 12.5 years from formal concept studies to its first flight (and 9 years from official program approval)","Space Shuttle was an even harder project. Amendments to the Starship project were all directed towards making it fly better. Modifications which various departments demanded from the Shuttle project made it a worse and worse spaceship. They even had to let go of the emergency recovery capability, which eventually led to death of 10 astronauts.",0
