   172	50528708,zurfer,11,False,Carnegie Mellon is putting computer science courses on YouTube for free,"I&#x27;m very open-minded about entertaining drastic societal changes but I just don&#x27;t think the facts support this prediction.<p>1. LLM performance has more or less peaked. Since Fable 5 released in June, every Claude model has been a ~1-2% improvement over the last one (if you go by benchmarks). Fable 5.2 marketing verbiage has shifted to how it&#x27;s &quot;lower cost per task&quot; which imo marks a regime shift. I would bet money that this time next year, we&#x27;re not going to have a model that&#x27;s obviously and significantly better than the best ones we have today.<p>2. If you hold that this is about as good as LLMs are gonna get, we haven&#x27;t found ourselves at a point where we can completely remove the software engineer from the seat. A software engineer is still required to create real commercial-quality software. The role hasn&#x27;t been commodified either - a better software engineer still writes better software.",How much do you want to bet? And what would be the decision criteria? Any benchmark that you trust?,1
   173	50528972,barrkel,29,False,Why 3dfx 1 cards had such a washed out color output?,"I dream of a parallel timeline when OKLAB became the standard colourspace in 1980! It&#x27;s a very colourful dream, the lightness stays consistent as the hues change, and colour interpolation just works!","Oklab&#x2F;oklch is good for consistent luminance across hue, but the real world gamut is so limited - our display &#x2F; print tech is always able to make green&#x2F;yellow far brighter than red&#x2F;blue, for any given tech - it can be a little frustrating to use.",1
   174	50529942,ciefa,31,False,Carnegie Mellon is putting computer science courses on YouTube for free,"Hi! I&#x27;m Phillip Compeau, I teach in Carnegie Mellon&#x27;s Griffin School of Computer Science, and I run CS Learn, which launched today.<p>We&#x27;re releasing full semesters of lectures from current CMU computer science courses across a variety of topics, with one course premiering each day this week. More courses come in January.<p>Yes, MIT got here 25 years ago :). What I&#x27;m excited about is how we&#x27;re doing it: a few of our students built tools that automate a lot of the production work, so a small team can get a course out quickly and still have people review every lecture.<p>We&#x27;ll have lots more coming across a wide array of subjects. I&#x27;d love to know if there are topics you would like to see us cover!","I love that there will be an updated version online now for such courses now.<p>Yes MIT did it 25 years ago or w&#x2F;e, but I&#x27;m sure _something_ has changed that is worth knowing about :D",0
