   178	50541234,spoaceman7777,12,False,Nvidia stopped disclosing Singapore sales as revenues hit $104B,"&gt; <i>this ongoing hatchet job</i><p>Let’s call a spade a spade. In your book, is a boss sleeping with a subordinate okay? Is it only okay if the boss makes lots of money?<p>Because there is a world of difference between criticizing an angle (where I’m sympathetic and criticizing the broader picture (where everyone outside Silicon Valley is horrified).","Personally, yeah, I think it is fine for a boss to sleep with a subordinate. It is the norm in the vast majority of the world, the main plot point of countless movies, and the starting point of an unfathomable number of happy relationships.<p>The basis on which this is considered a moral travesty is built upon about half a decade of neuroses that have built up in only one single very specific corner of this planet that has 8.4B people living on it, and if you really believe that the yawning gulf between the power of a CEO and that of a multi-multi-millionaire VP of a multi-trillion dollar company is such an unfathomably dark and twisted power differential that it is our moral imperative to torpedo both the US economy and national security to correct it, then your mind is broken beyond any hope of repair.",1
   179	50542574,fsh,8,False,Why is Gemini 4 Carbon taking so long?,"It&#x27;s pretty obvious that Gemini 4 Carbon is not token efficient. It also has relatively low tokens-per-second compared to other models. I can think of several reasons why this is the case:<p>1. Google has huge amounts of cash and compute. Their goal was to train a model that that has the highest quality &amp; frontier performance, not create a model that&#x27;s efficient to run and serve. They have other models for that, like Hydrogen.<p>2. Google created Carbon without caring for inference costs. DeepMind is subsidized by the rest of Google&#x27;s empire. Selling inference is an insignificant part of Google&#x27;s revenue. But for Anthropic and OpenAI, selling inference is their entire business model. Anthropic and OpenAI have enormous incentive to sell as much inference as they can in the most efficient manner. Google doesn&#x27;t have this incentive.<p>3. They are one of the few companies that can be inefficient. Out of all the frontier labs, Google DeepMind is probably the least constrained by compute. Chinese labs have around 10 to 100 times less compute, so they must release highly optimized models.",4. Google did a worse job than Anthropic and OpenAI.,1
   180	50545128,zombot,73,False,AI is killing All About Berlin,"People are being conditioned to think that all the answers come from AI, or that AI knows everything.<p>But AI is only getting (inaccurate shadows of) the info from these sources which it is simultaneously working to kill. (Websites like this, StackOverflow, human written code)<p>What happens when there are no more sources and everyone just expects AI to answer everything or write everything? Recursive self-improvement? I doubt it.",&gt; What happens when there are no more sources<p>They just let the LLMs hallucinate and lie to us. It&#x27;s not like you could verify anything anymore because fact-reporting organizations have been killed off by then too.,1
   181	50545770,goobatrooba,35,False,Quad9's withdrawal from France: A canary in the coal mine,"&gt; isn&#x27;t three a need for technical solutions?<p>I don&#x27;t think anyone&#x27;s saying that technical solutions are by definition unnecessary. Whether or not it is, is just not relevant to the matter at hand.<p>Roads facilitate crime. Opposing the removal&#x2F;blocking of roads is not approving of crime, and it&#x27;s not on the opposer to propose a better solution to crime. The cost of blocking roads easily outweighs the benefits of reduced crime, and that is all that is needed to decide not to do it.",the roads aren&#x27;t a service provider and can&#x27;t selectively filter specific cars. But we do in Europe have low emissions zones were people with high emissions vehicles and thus without the right plate or stickers do indeed get fines.,1
